-- A refinement written in an `interface` METHOD SIGNATURE enforces nothing.
-- Nothing in `lib/refinecheck` reads `A.method_decl`'s `md_ty`: the pass's
-- `DInterface` arm descends only into a method's DEFAULT body, and the two
-- other walks that touch `iface_methods` read method NAMES only. Nor does the
-- front end carry it anywhere -- `Desugar.inject_defaults` synthesises a
-- default method's `fn_def` with `fn_ret_ty = None` and parameters taken from
-- the default lambda, which carry no `param_ty`, so even that one path drops
-- the predicate on the floor.
--
-- So the contract obliges no call site AND lets no body assume anything: a
-- MISSING check, not an unsound one. Until Task 5 of the 2026-07-30
-- refinement backlog it was also SILENT, which is the failure mode this area
-- keeps producing -- it parses, typechecks, and reads exactly like a working
-- contract. It now emits a WARNING naming the method and the `impl`-side
-- spelling that works.
--
-- This is an `accept/` witness on purpose, and its exit code is the point.
-- A warning does not change `--check`'s exit status, so this program must
-- stay exit 0: making an interface-signature refinement a hard ERROR is a
-- separate decision (it would break code that compiles today), and making it
-- actually ENFORCE is a much larger one. This witness guards against doing
-- either by accident. The warning TEXT is pinned in `test/test_refinecheck.ml`'s
-- `interface-signature-refinement` suite instead -- `check_types.sh` asserts
-- only exit 0 for `accept/`, and pins substrings for `reject/` alone.
--
-- The remedy the warning names is deliberately stated for BOTH positions,
-- since they are enforced under different conditions: a RETURN refinement on
-- an `impl` method is always checked (`check_fn_post` runs unconditionally),
-- while a PARAMETER refinement is enforced only when the method name is
-- adoptable. `Bumper`/`Box` below is the working spelling alongside the inert
-- one, so this file witnesses the contrast rather than only the defect.
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md, "A refinement in an interface signature
-- enforces nothing".
mod T137 do

  -- INERT: the predicate here is never read by the refinement checker.
  interface Runner(a) do
    fn run : a -> {Int | _ > 0} -> Int
  end

  type Box = Box(Int)

  -- ENFORCED: the same predicate on the `impl` method's own parameter. `bump`
  -- is adoptable (exactly one impl defines it, no top-level `fn` shares the
  -- name), so callers are obliged and the body may assume it.
  -- The interface signature stays UNREFINED (so this half warns nothing); the
  -- refinement goes on the impl method, which the typechecker accepts against
  -- the plain `Int` in the interface. `bump(Box(1), 0 - 5)` is a refinement
  -- ERROR, which is the difference between this half and the one above.
  interface Bumper(a) do
    fn bump : a -> Int -> Int
  end

  impl Bumper(Box) do
    fn bump(_b : Box, n : {Int | _ > 0}) : Int do n end
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    bump(Box(1), 5)
  end

end
